WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.290
Superhero movies are obviously fun to watch—their
continued success at the box office is proof

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.880
enough that audiences get a kick out of them.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.080
Maybe it's because they've got a little something
for everyone—plenty of action, high-stakes

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.470
human drama, and even a few laughs laced into
the mix.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:21.340
As it happens, there are often even more laughs
during filming than there are onscreen.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.490
It's probably a combination of the set being
a high-tech playground and everyone getting

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:30.619
punchy from long shooting days, but whatever
the case, it makes for some really silly and

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:33.310
amusing superhero movie bloopers.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:34.430
Daredevil

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:40.130
He may be blind, but Daredevil has other abilities
and heightened senses that make up for it.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:45.370
Not among those abilities, it would seem,
is dominion over the sky and its many creatures—as

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:50.220
Ben Affleck discovered while filming a fight
scene in 2003's Daredevil.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.860
While trying to perform his well-rehearsed
stunt choreography, he found himself unable

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.860
to quite pull it off because the birds that
were supposed to make the scene look cool

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.420
just kept running into him.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.450
"Ugh!

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.480
Uh.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.640
Dude, I couldn't…"

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.250
Superman

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.720
It's a risky business to employ child actors
in big budget movies, where saying the right

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.320
thing at the right time is kind of important.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:25.210
In a scene from 1978's Superman, Jonathan
and Martha Kent have discovered toddler Superman

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.120
and are debating what to do with the presumably
lost or orphaned boy.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.280
That's when the three-year-old thespian forgets
he's supposed to be acting ...

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.210
"We are going to find this boy's proper family."

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.270
"Oh, he hasn't got any.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:38.270
Not around here, anyway."

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.530
"Yeah I do!"

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.030
Spider-Man 3

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.030
Spider-Man isn't cool.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:47.390
Or at least Peter Parker, Spider-Man's mild-mannered
alter ego, isn't cool.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:52.409
He's certainly no Bruce Wayne — he's lacking
the Dark Knight's suave self-possession.

00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:57.170
That's why this blooper reel, in which Spider-Man
3 star Tobey Maguire is completely unable

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:03.690
to stop laughing while filming a normal, plot-building,
mundane scene, almost feels like it's in character—having

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:08.530
a laughing fit in a restaurant is so goofy,
it seems like something young, awkward Peter

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:09.750
Parker would do.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.760
"We got it under control."

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:15.760
"Action."

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.860
"Hi, Peter!"

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:17.460
"Hi!"

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.340
Ant-Man

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:26.670
Of course there are going to be some bloopers,
riffs, outtakes, and cut scenes from a superhero

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.420
movie starring Paul Rudd.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:34.110
He's a gifted, veteran comic actor, and he
brought a sense of fun and play to 2015's

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:39.480
Ant-Man—a movie that, at the time, was based
around one of Marvel's less mainstream characters.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:45.230
Rudd, who usually stars in wild comedies and
small dramas, can't help but reflect on how

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.740
he's suddenly wearing a superhero suit in
a mega-budget action movie ...

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.069
"You talkin' to me?

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:54.400
You must be talkin' to me, cuz I'm the only
one here."

00:02:58.550 --> 00:02:59.760
Deadpool

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:06.230
The dialogue is so self-referential and self-deprecating,
and Ryan Reynolds has such a witty, off-the-cuff

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:12.370
delivery, that almost the entirety of 2016's
Deadpool feels like it could have been improvised.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.069
That's not to say that Reynolds didn't improvise
on set, because he did.

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:17.310
A lot.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:21.620
One particular moment, though, seems to have
caught his costars completely off guard.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:26.480
After all, it's in the middle of a fight scene
in the back of a speeding car—not generally

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.670
the type of place where a person breaks out
into an old Kenny Rogers song...

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:36.660
"Know when to fold 'em / Know when to walk
away / Know when to run…"

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:38.819
Spider-Man

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:43.330
Willem Dafoe is one of the finest dramatic
actors of his or any generation.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:48.150
That doesn't mean he doesn't like to have
fun, as evidenced by his decision to play

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:51.050
the Green Goblin in 2002's Spider-Man.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.900
He also likes to have fun riffing on set.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.239
When filming what's supposed to be a dramatic,
tension-building moment in which the villain's

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:04.300
ire for Spider-Man threatens to boil over,
Dafoe started making a lot of angry noises—and

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.400
then some other noises ...

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.700
*OINK OINK OINK*

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.470
"Spider-Man!"

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.240
"Cut!"

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.700
Guardians of the Galaxy

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.849
There are a few ways for an actor to react
if their scene partner messes up.

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:23.340
They can remain silent, go back to their mark,
and try again.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:24.979
They can also laugh uproariously.

00:04:24.979 --> 00:04:28.340
Or they can stay in character and run with
the gag.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.930
On the set of Guardians of the Galaxy, Michael
Rooker went with the latter option ...

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:33.930
"He was cargo!

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.240
And you boys are going soft on him."

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.700
"You walked us right off the set!"

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.069
"Yes, I did.

00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:42.340
And I'm gonna do it again!"

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.340
Kick-Ass 2

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:49.089
Chloë Grace Moretz is a young actress, but
she's been working a long time and was already

00:04:49.089 --> 00:04:54.979
a seasoned professional when she made 2013's
Kick-Ass 2, reprising her role as self-made

00:04:54.979 --> 00:04:57.009
superhero Hit Girl.

00:04:57.009 --> 00:05:01.529
In one outtake, Moretz delivers a line with
such conviction that nobody but the script

00:05:01.529 --> 00:05:04.980
supervisor would've even known she'd muffed
it…

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.940
"Pull over, s--- breath.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:10.879
Wrong line."

00:05:10.879 --> 00:05:11.879
X-Men

00:05:11.879 --> 00:05:17.460
The first X-Men movie was released in 2000,
long before the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:22.449
was up and running with its various spinoffs,
team-ups, sequels, and crossovers.

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:27.339
Before that, superhero movies were generally
standalone affairs featuring just one comic

00:05:27.339 --> 00:05:28.339
property.

00:05:28.339 --> 00:05:33.090
So it's really silly and surprising when a
guy dressed as a certain web-slinger sneaks

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:35.869
into the lineup of super-powered mutants,
ready to fight ...

00:05:35.869 --> 00:05:40.800
"I am completely in the wrong movie.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.660
I am so sorry."

00:05:44.289 --> 00:05:45.680
Thanks for watching!

00:05:45.689 --> 00:05:48.720
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.389
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

